{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An invocation-specific plan binding one explicitly selected Product Definition, the complete companion population of one exact upstream release-matched cohort, and owner-selected consumer bindings. Product Definition owns composition; cohort and Product release records own release and member identity. Invocation selection, plan and result are operation evidence, not a persistent dependency declaration, registry or lock.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":368,
          "quote":"A Complete Consumer Update Plan binds one explicitly selected Product\nDefinition and the complete companion population of one exact upstream\nrelease-matched cohort to the consumer bindings selected by the\ninvocation's owner.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line":361
        }
      ],
      "key":"complete_consumer_update_plan",
      "label":"Complete Consumer Update Plan"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The separate cohort-update operation presents a complete plan without consumer effects and, upon the prescribed acceptance and checks, applies the selected consumer update. Its scope remains distinct from the narrower adopt, sync and fleet contracts.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":376,
          "quote":"The separate `cohort-update` operation presents the whole plan without\nconsumer effects.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76b1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line":375
        }
      ],
      "key":"cohort_update",
      "label":"Complete consumer update operation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Manager outcomes are predicate-specific refinements of the referenced core result algebra, adding no core value. Effect outcomes concern whether the intended external effect occurred; admission or construction outcomes concern permission or candidate availability; effectless commands report their own evaluated predicates.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":420,
          "quote":"each\nfor its own predicate; the cause of an outcome selects its value, and the\nmapping adds no core value (P5, P6):",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F4.%20Shared%20installed%20release%20basis%20and%20toolchain%20manager%2FExplicit%20complete%20consumer%20update",
          "start_line":418
        }
      ],
      "key":"manager_result_domains",
      "label":"Manager result domains"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"PRODUCT_DEFINITION declares ownership of the semantic-resolution bounded context urn:stdo:bounded-context:semantic-resolution under the selected complete STDO basis. A glossary may index that identity but does not declare it.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":493,
          "quote":"This member owns this law in the semantic-resolution bounded context,\n`urn:stdo:bounded-context:semantic-resolution`, under the selected complete\nSTDO basis.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":490
        }
      ],
      "key":"semantic_resolution_context",
      "label":"Semantic-resolution bounded context"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A term is a lexical label in an occurrence, not a context-free concept identity. Every material occurrence has the semantic address (term, bounded-context identity, owning authority, selected basis, governed scope).",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":496,
          "quote":"A term is a lexical label used in an occurrence, not a context-free concept\nidentity.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":495
        },
        {
          "end_line":512,
          "quote":"(term, bounded-context identity, owning authority, selected basis, governed scope)",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":512
        }
      ],
      "key":"term_occurrence",
      "label":"Term occurrence and semantic address"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An explicitly identified semantic scope in which governed vocabulary, concepts and relations are jointly interpretable under named authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":498,
          "quote":"A bounded context is an explicitly identified semantic scope in\nwhich a governed vocabulary, concept set and relation set are jointly\ninterpretable under named authority.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":496
        }
      ],
      "key":"bounded_context",
      "label":"Bounded context"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An exact, resolvable reference locating a meaning owned by an authority under a selected basis; it creates neither meaning nor authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":501,
          "quote":"A concept reference is an exact,\nresolvable reference to a meaning owned by an authority under a selected\nbasis; the reference locates that meaning and mints neither the meaning nor\nits authority",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":498
        }
      ],
      "key":"concept_reference",
      "label":"Concept reference"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An explicit, owner-authorised relation between exact concepts in bounded contexts. Its four kinds are Semantic Import, Semantic Translation, Semantic Equivalence and Semantic Disambiguation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":561,
          "quote":"A cross-context relation is explicit and owner-authorised. Its carrier\nstates (P1, P6, P4b):",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":560
        },
        {
          "end_line":597,
          "quote":"These four kinds are related to the typed relations that",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":597
        }
      ],
      "key":"cross_context_relation",
      "label":"Cross-context semantic relation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Semantic Import applies the FOUNDATIONS import definition to a declared target scope, preserving unchanged meaning, direction, loss and lack of authority. Semantic Translation declares directional meaning change and loss and is not an import. Semantic Equivalence requires governing authorities to establish equality while each concept retains its owner and context; neither spelling nor opposing translations proves it. Semantic Disambiguation selects one meaning for one scope without equating competitors; a selected foreign concept enters only through a declared import, translation or equivalence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":593,
          "quote":"| Semantic Disambiguation | not an import: it selects one meaning for one scope and does not make competing concepts equal. Where the selected concept is owned in another bounded context, it enters that scope only by the import, translation or equivalence its carrier declares (§6) |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":593
        },
        {
          "end_line":592,
          "quote":"| Semantic Equivalence | not an import: each concept keeps its own owner and context, and neither adopts the other's meaning. It requires the governing authorities to establish the claimed equality; neither identical spelling nor two opposing translations proves it automatically |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":592
        }
      ],
      "key":"semantic_relation_kinds",
      "label":"Four semantic relation kinds"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A directional translation preserving declared source meaning while narrowing admissible instances or adding target-context constraints. It identifies the exact target, preserved and narrowed meaning, excluded source instances, and absence or law of an inverse; the label alone establishes nothing.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":609,
          "quote":"A specialisation is a directional translation whose target preserves the\ndeclared source meaning while narrowing its admissible instances or adding\ntarget-context constraints.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":604
        }
      ],
      "key":"semantic_specialisation",
      "label":"Semantic specialisation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"GLOSSARY is a non-deciding locator index whose records point to exact source clauses declaring contexts and owning concepts. It defines neither, repairs no missing declaration and creates no shared default; a concept applies only through the semantic address or explicit target-context import.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":558,
          "quote":"The glossary (the [GLOSSARY](GLOSSARY.md) member) is a non-deciding locator index.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F5.%20Bounded-context%20semantic%20resolution",
          "start_line":553
        }
      ],
      "key":"glossary_locator_index",
      "label":"Glossary locator index"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Local axiom, override and disambiguation bindings locate source-owned decisions and lawful authority. Overlay listing and file category confer no authority; metadata makes decisions discoverable and checkable without restating meanings or creating decisions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":615,
          "quote":"A local axiom, override or disambiguation gains no authority from its file\ncategory or from being listed in the overlay.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F6.%20Local%20constitutional%20binding",
          "start_line":613
        }
      ],
      "key":"local_constitution_binding",
      "label":"Local constitutional binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A Product-owned accepted declaration of the shared frame set for finite actors engaging one governed Product scope or outcome. It binds exact reference-frame law and optional profile basis; admitting authorities and scopes; frames and evaluation inventory; actor-binding rules and capability envelopes; semantic, evaluation, operation and decision grants; result, conjunction, translation and coverage relations; lifecycle triggers and invalidation law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":652,
          "quote":"A Project Reference-Frame Basis is the Product-owned accepted declaration\nof the shared frame set through which finite actors collectively engage one\ngoverned Product scope or outcome",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F7.%20Collective%20reference-frame%20basis",
          "start_line":649
        }
      ],
      "key":"project_reference_frame_basis",
      "label":"Project Reference-Frame Basis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An execution-scoped binding of one agent to an applicable frame declaration or active configuration, exact evaluation, subject, basis, evidence boundary and capability envelope. An authorised ticket or work instruction carries the activation packet referenced to REFERENCE_FRAME §11; the step is referenced to ACTIVATION §1. This differs from the durable collective basis.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":702,
          "quote":"An Agent Frame\nActivation is the execution-scoped binding of one agent to an applicable\nframe declaration or active configuration, an exact evaluation, subject,\nbasis, evidence boundary and capability envelope;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F7.%20Collective%20reference-frame%20basis",
          "start_line":695
        }
      ],
      "key":"agent_frame_activation",
      "label":"Agent Frame Activation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"PRODUCT_DEFINITION owns urn:stdo:bounded-context:build-tenancy under the selected complete basis. Build tenancy relates one constitutional WHAT to one or more independent project-owned HOW realisations; one tenant is the singleton case and multiple independent realisations constitute multi-build-tenancy. Build-tenant and binding concepts are explicitly owned by SPEC_METHOD §11; the multi-tenant work-item relation is owned by TICKETS §4.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":758,
          "quote":"In this context build tenancy is that one-or-more relation; one tenant is\nits singleton case, and multi-build-tenancy begins when the same WHAT has\nmore than one independent realisation",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F8.%20WHAT%2C%20HOW%20and%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line":755
        }
      ],
      "key":"build_tenancy",
      "label":"Build tenancy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The WHAT locators identify current constitutional Intent, Product and specification surfaces. The Tenant Registry is canonical how.build_tenants, each stable tenant locating root, design and implementation. how.common binds shared realisation law explicitly adopted by more than one tenant. Work locators identify Goals, durable ticket root and backlog/active/completed lanes, commentary root and optional sprint manifests; Goals own the wave, ticket authority owns state and comments remain non-authoritative.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":768,
          "quote":"The Tenant Registry is the canonical `how.build_tenants` collection for the\nproduct's independent HOW realisations.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F8.%20WHAT%2C%20HOW%20and%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line":765
        },
        {
          "end_line":784,
          "quote":"this section binds where each carrier kind lives.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F8.%20WHAT%2C%20HOW%20and%20work%20carriers",
          "start_line":784
        }
      ],
      "key":"what_how_work_locators",
      "label":"WHAT, HOW and work locators"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Recursive discovery locates stdo_*.json within the selected workspace boundary, including root and arbitrarily nested project roots. Explicit directed composition, rather than nesting, relates independent definitions through target locator, stable expected definition identity, relation authority and non-empty governing contracts.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":791,
          "quote":"Tools discover `stdo_*.json` recursively within the selected workspace\nboundary: definitions at the workspace root and definitions at arbitrarily\ndeep nested project roots",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F9.%20Recursive%20discovery%20and%20explicit%20composition",
          "start_line":788
        }
      ],
      "key":"product_discovery_composition",
      "label":"Product discovery and explicit composition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Minimum semantic-resolution conformance cases under an exact selected basis cover unique use and material collision: Frame distinguishes evaluation context from runtime carrier; Owner distinguishes specialist family from semantic or decision authority; Product distinguishes immutable release from mutable definition; Tenant distinguishes build realisation from hosted/customer/account/runtime/data tenancy; User remains unresolved among Product role, human, principal, account, actor, operator and data subject until downstream authority defines or relates meanings.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":863,
          "quote":"Conformance covers both uniquely resolved use and material collision. At\nminimum it establishes these cases under an exact selected basis",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F10.%20Semantic-resolution%20conformance%20cases",
          "start_line":861
        },
        {
          "end_line":871,
          "quote":"| `User` | a Product role, human person, identity principal, account, actor, operator or data subject | unresolved until downstream authority defines or explicitly relates the applicable meanings |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F10.%20Semantic-resolution%20conformance%20cases",
          "start_line":871
        }
      ],
      "key":"semantic_resolution_conformance",
      "label":"Semantic-resolution conformance cases"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"STDO-SURFACE-001 describes modular authorship with singular authority: each law has one owning member, consumed elsewhere by reference, and members become operative only as one complete released version. A release identifies exact members, digests, review basis, human acceptance and immutable identity. Method law owns normative algebra, authority, abstract states/transitions, admissibility, necessary causal order and evidence invariants; consumers own concrete realisation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":889,
          "quote":"STDO-SURFACE-001. The shared methodology is modular in authorship and\nsingular in authority. Each law has one owning member; other members\nconsume it by reference.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F11.%20One%20constitutional%20surface%20and%20version%20boundary",
          "start_line":885
        }
      ],
      "key":"constitutional_method_surface",
      "label":"One constitutional method surface"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Bootstrap starts every method-defined product from the selected cut's PRODUCT_DEFINITION_TEMPLATE.json, copied to its definition root as stdo_default.json for a singleton default project or stdo_<label>.json for a named definition. Its starter topology places Goals, Intent, Product and requirements under specification/, common/default realisation under build_tenants/, and sprint, ticket and comment carriers under .ai-workspace/. These are defaults selected or replaced through URI bindings.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":952,
          "quote":"Every product defined under this method begins from\n`templates/PRODUCT_DEFINITION_TEMPLATE.json`, copied to the product's\ndefinition root as `stdo_default.json` for a singleton default project or\nas `stdo_<label>.json` for a named product definition.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F12.%20Bootstrap",
          "start_line":946
        }
      ],
      "key":"product_bootstrap",
      "label":"Product bootstrap and starter topology"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The PRODUCT_DEFINITION source-disposition ledger reports received rc.2 SPEC_METHOD rows as conserved at §§1–12. Routes are provenance only; member-name carrying is not itself a disposition. Reported carryings include IDENTITY_METHOD→IDENTITY, RELEASE_METHOD→RELEASE, GLOSSARY_GUIDE→GLOSSARY, REFERENCE_FRAME_METHOD→REFERENCE_FRAME and STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE→Executive baseline; accepted design ontology wording and mutable source paths are generalised without claimed loss. New material includes the §1 family table, cause-sensitive manager result mapping and semantic-relation readings. These are source claims, not verified conservation results.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1096,
          "quote":"Every row\nis restated in this member's own text; a route is provenance only. Where\nthe predecessor named one of its own members, the tenant member that\nreceives the same law is named instead; this is carrying, not a\ndisposition, and is recorded in the row.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#PRODUCT_DEFINITION%2F13.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":1092
        }
      ],
      "key":"product_definition_source_ledger",
      "label":"Product Definition source-disposition ledger"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"PROFILES is tier 3 and owns the core/profile boundary, declared-binding rule and profile import rule, while indexing profiles. Profiles are domain or operational specialisations; tiers 1 and 2 are the core.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":7,
          "quote":"A profile is a domain or\noperational specialisation of the method; the members of tiers 1 and 2\nare the core.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES",
          "start_line":5
        }
      ],
      "key":"profiles_member",
      "label":"Profiles and core boundary"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Within PROFILES, binding means the profile-declared binding of a core mechanism with direction, preserved meaning, narrowed scope and invalidation. It is distinct from Product adoption binding in PRODUCT_DEFINITION §7 and typed-contract carrier binding in UX §7. The rule is described as new, with provenance in ODD's graph-native specialisations of F_P and F_H.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":24,
          "quote":"In this member \"binding\" means\nthat declared binding of a core mechanism; a Product's adoption binding is\n[PRODUCT_DEFINITION §7](PRODUCT_DEFINITION.md#7-collective-reference-frame-basis)'s,\nand a carrier binding to a typed contract is [UX §7](UX.md#7-carrier-binding)'s;\nthe three are not interchanged.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES%2F1.%20The%20boundary",
          "start_line":20
        }
      ],
      "key":"profile_declared_binding",
      "label":"Profile-declared binding"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The graph-execution index names two tier-3 members. ODD reports all 48 predecessor rows and binds P0's bounded step to one vector or edge traversal; graph-native F_P/F_D/F_H shorthands, F_P/F_H specialisations of SPEC_METHOD §5; and ACTIVATION §4 graph-function refinement with §11.8 cumulative environment as a specialised earlier-output contract. TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE offers an optional closed model-family signature over one exact a_c basis for immutable histories over a mutable subject, OP-001–OP-007, reporting 25 conserved rows and one proposed supersession pending the Product owner. ODD §10 owns runtime/product/service responsibility layers; worker internal solution strategy stays outside the framework.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":59,
          "quote":"Graph execution is two tier-3 members, indexed here; each restates its\npredecessor in full in its own text and declares its bindings under §1",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES%2F2.%20Graph%20execution",
          "start_line":57
        },
        {
          "end_line":64,
          "quote":"| [TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE](TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE.md) | rc.2 TRAVERSAL_OCCURRENCE_PROFILE, all 26 rows, 25 conserved and 1 proposed superseded, pending the product owner | an optional closed model-family signature over one exact `a_c` basis of AXIOMATIC_CALCULUS for immutable occurrence histories over a mutable subject, with OP-001 to OP-007 |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation/claude/9aee01ffa76cfbde1a6b8f1153a25bd6d71c216e/source/standards/PROFILES.md#PROFILES%2F2.%20Graph%20execution",
          "start_line":64
        }
      ],
      "key":"graph_execution_profiles",
      "label":"Graph-execution profile index"
    },
